I thought I would speak about 20 or 30  minutes I'd like to describe for  lawsuits that are in various stages of  litigation tell you what you're likely  to see coming out of the Supreme Court  and ongoing litigation over the next  couple of three years but at the  suggestion of a few people want to just  start out by telling you how it is I got  in the this sort of sordid business of  kind of undeviating us up by race then  that particular phrase some of you may  recognize from Chief Justice John  Roberts in a case that was decided a few  years ago and that advanced the ball in  ending race-based preferences in K  through 12 he said this is a sordid  business divvying us up by race and  indeed it is a sordid business my  background is not in the law no I am NOT  a lawyer I am NOT a social scientist  although I am a fellow at the American  Enterprise Institute and have been there  now for almost ten years my story is a  little unusual I got into this simply  out of a small miracle that took place  in 1989 and that was my daughter was  accepted to college and um my wife and I  at the time we're living in a garden  variety Houston suburb I was in the  investment business she was in the  insurance business and when our daughter  left home we decided we no longer really  wanted to live in the suburbs we wanted  to live in kind of inner-city Houston i  know david has spent some time in  houston we wanted to move close to the  museum district and your rice university  yet so i think it's a common pattern for  professionals to want to leave the  suburbs after the kids for you can move  closer to this really downtown action  and we did that bought a house  and I went to vote for the first time in  1990 in my new neighborhood and realized  looked at the ballot and realized that  the Republican Party had not fielded a  candidate to run against the incumbent  Democrat no I really wasn't a political  animal but that bothered me that area  everyone should have a choice in  virtually all of their candidate races  so I called the Republican Party of  Houston harris county and i was told  well you know you live in a primarily  minority district and you know  republicans just aren't going to win in  a district like this and that seemed  like a reasonable explanation for  someone who was in the investment  business but the more I thought about it  the more peeved I got and assorted to  cut to the chase two years later I have  won the republican primary to represent  the 18th congressional district of texas  i was now I was now thick in the thick  in electoral politics and faced you know  a very well-known african-american  Democrat incumbent it was an interesting  race because I'm Jewish and I'm  conservative and you know normally that  is not something you find a race like  this is not you know it's not very  typical at least it wasn't back in 1992  needless to say campaign contributions  were not rolling in to support us  so we had to default to what used to be  called simple retail politics or those  of you of a certain age will know before  the internet candidates used to go door  to door knocking on doors and handing  out campaign literature and that's  exactly what my wife and I and all of  our volunteers did I would be on one  side of a garden-variety Houston Street  knocking on doors people would still  answer doors back then by the way and my  wife would be on the other side and I  would go up to a household that I knew  was now in this newly configured 18th  congressional district right after  redistricting and the homeowner would  have a sign for a different  congressional race there and this just  didn't happen once or twice there were  dozens perhaps hundreds of yard signs  promoting candidates that weren't the  candidate of the homeowner and I would  have to explain that you know  congressman bill archer is no longer  your congressman your new congressman is  congressman Craig Washington and I'm the  guy that's running against him and the  reason we had to do this was that block  by block the legislature of the state of  Texas had using what was now in 1992  fairly advanced computer mapping systems  overlap with census data they've  identified almost house-by-house the  race and ethnicity of each voter and  then harvested that voter or that  household are really that census block  into racially homogeneous voting  districts so that if on one sign of kind  of this garden variety street there were  three or four african-american families  or maybe a small apartment complex with  three or four african-american families  that side of the street would go into  one congressional district across the  street if there were two or three  Hispanic families or maybe a similar  apartment  flex with Hispanics that would now go  into a new Hispanic district and the  same thing with you know white families  so multiracial multi-ethnic  neighborhoods were split apart block by  block to create these sprawling bug  splat districts that would go from you  know 80 miles you you've been if you  haven't been to Houston then just  imagine wasps Angeles a little smaller a  little smaller you know a district would  would spread out in one direction for 40  50 miles another direction two or three  miles another direct so that when you  looked at the the silhouette of the  district that I was running to represent  you really couldn't tell if someone  asked you a question you couldn't and  said I live in the the stonington  neighborhood of Central Texas you you  simply couldn't tell that person what  congressional district he or she was  drawn into now this is this is an old  story racial gerrymandering and  gerrymandering in general has been  around but um because of these sort of  technological improvements the state of  Texas along with a lot of other states  north carolina virginia South Carolina  Florida they went through the same  process and created these these these  were shot like districts so you kind of  probably know what happened i didn't  serve in Congress i i i i i washed the  election i ran 10 points ahead of george  herbert walker bush and that was that  was somewhat gratifying in that the the  following election cycle my opponent was  challenged in the Democrat primary and  lost so uh but I this this house by  house disintegration of neighborhoods  that used to be kept together for the  purposes of elective representatives  that bothered me  lots of things bother me yeah and and um  I guess about five or six months after  the election I read about a lawsuit that  had been filed in North Carolina that uh  that had challenged a similar district  that was drawn to connect  african-american neighborhoods and and  and part of the district included just  one highway 1 interstate interstate 85 i  believe the connected the  african-american neighborhoods in  Charlotte with the african-american  neighborhoods I don't know 85 miles away  using a highway just to connect those  two districts so I thought well if they  can file a lawsuit in North Carolina  I'll file a lawsuit in Texas now I had  not what I had never been sued before in  my life I had never sued anyone so here  I was you know in a position of you know  in the investment business needing to  hire a legal team to challenge the state  of Texas redistricting policy excuse me  I reached into my own pocket took out  about eight thousand dollars a month  found a real estate loiter in Monroe  Louisiana who had some experience at  this now the reason I had to go to  Monroe Louisiana is because no lawyer at  any large law firm in the state of Texas  or even small law firm in the state of  Texas thought that I had a chance of  winning or frankly even the folks in  North Carolina had a chance of winning  but i found one guy i pulled together  some friends we recruited sort of this  ragtag team of pro bono lawyers and we  sued the state of texas arguing that the  texas congressional redistricting plan  was an unconstitutional gerrymander  under the Fourteenth Amendment of the  United States we drew a three-judge  panel  we had a one-week trial what we had off  are three or four lawyers at our table  the United States intervene and had like  DOJ lawyer there must have been 50  lawyers on the other side and lo and  behold about five months after the trial  we won ah the three-judge panel in the  state of Texas declared that the Texas  congressional redistricting plan it was  as it was enacted for the 1992 election  was unconstitutional that was a  wonderful victory but then something  unusual happened that no one anticipated  and that was that the state of Texas  appealed to the United States Supreme  Court in order to defend their district  and lo and behold the Supreme Court took  it up so now I have gone from you know  an amateur congressional candidate and  an amateur plaintiff to now trying to  put together a professional team to  argue this case at the Supreme Court  which we did we made arguments I guess I  guess about eight months after the court  took it up and a few months after that  lo and behold we won that case style  Bush versus Vera in a five-to-four  opinion authored by Justice Sandra Day  O'Connor during the course of all of  this my phone started to ring from other  people around the country who were in um  you know similar situations people who  had learned about the case obviously  through the Supreme Court and I asked  one of my expert witnesses to compile a  list for me of the most racially  gerrymandered congressional districts in  the country down to the least racially  gerrymandered districts in the country  and I i took this winning legal team  that now had finally gotten paid from  the from the state of texas that's part  of the part of the beauty of suing under  certain provisions of the Voting Rights  Act and we will we took this show on the  road I I recruited plaintiffs in New  York Virginia South Carolina Louisiana  and Florida and we just started suing  and lo and behold over the course of  about I'd say about four years we struck  down the congressional redistricting  plans of all of those states and and  here's what here's here's what here's  what everyone gained from that although  the states were compelled to redraw  their congressional districts and they  did and i am not going to tell you that  these districts were perfect squares and  circles the these these busted apart  neighborhoods for the most part were put  back together so that multiracial  multi-ethnic neighborhoods pardon me for  the most part now have this commonality  they have a congress person and it  doesn't matter whether you're white  black or hispanic in in these  neighborhoods any longer you can come  together as Americans and as community  members to influence to influence that  that that representative and that  happened you know in all of those states  so I thought that was a that was a great  you know a great beginning to all of  this the day that I won my first lawsuit  at the Supreme Court my business  partners at the investment bank  I was working at identified that day as  the day that my interest in the  investment business collapsed and the  day that I decided that I was going to  become a part time amateur litigator and  that is indeed what is what what what  has happened over the last 20 years my  phone continued to ring from parents  whose children had been denied admission  to a magnet school program in Florida or  Texas or Louisiana oh thank you i needed  that that's good and i started to put  together legal teams that would go out  and represent parents and represent  individuals challenging race-based  policies whether it pertain to  educational issues additional voting  issues contracting issues or employment  issues since that initial case i have  had three cases argued at the Supreme  Court won a case out of Texas in which  we challenged one of the provisions of  the Voting Rights Act called Northwest  Austin Municipal Utility District number  one versus holder better known as num  bud know the blood no was argued at the  Supreme Court in 2008 and we won that  case an opinion offered by Chief Justice  Roberts that gave us a win but it didn't  really give us much of a win it it  alerted Congress that the the provision  of the Voting Rights Act that we  challenged and Manning I think  encapsulated it well Wow in 1965 it was  important for the deep south to be put  under federal supervision because of the  invidious  disenfranchisement of african-americans  there by 2006 these provisions had  morphed into something very different  than what they were used for in 1965 now  they became the very gerrymandering tool  that allowed and encouraged states like  Texas in New York and Virginia to create  these bugs blatt districts so we won the  mud no but we didn't win much during the  course of litigating the mud no I  started reaching out to other  jurisdictions and found Shelby County  Alabama they were a perfect jurisdiction  to sort of put the second chop on the  log we won we won some you know  important legal doctrines with the first  case but I knew it would take a second  one and that case was filed in 2000 and  late 2010 and an opinion in that case  came down in June of 2013 which  basically struck down these provisions  so this provision of the Voting Rights  Act that was used in very mischievous  ways to separate us by race into  different voting districts that that has  now come to an end and my opponents will  never forgive me for that I want to just  sort of briefly talk about college  admissions because this affects all of  us because we all have children and  grandchildren and nieces and nephews in  in 2003 when the Supreme Court allowed  the use of racial classifications in two  cases from the state of Michigan that  may I know Manny was involved in writing  amicus briefs with that those cases were  really were grave disappointments for  those of us who believe that your race  and ethnicity should not be something  that's used to either help you gain  admission to college or harm you in your  admissions prospects the Supreme Court  allowed I think what they described as  the delicate use of race in in  college admissions the day that opinion  came out the University of Texas held a  news conference and announced that they  at the University of Texas who had been  forbidden from using race and ethnicity  in their admissions process was now  going to add race-based affirmative  action on top of what everyone  recognized was a successful race neutral  means of achieving diversity at UT that  successful race neutral means was a  statute passed during the Bush  administration that allowed any student  at any high school in Texas who  graduated in the top 10% of his or her  class to attend any school in the state  and a year or two after that statute  became law the number of racial  minorities at the University of Texas  exceeded the number of racial minorities  that were admitted with affirmative  action so let me make sure you  understand this Texas was precluded from  using race and ethnicity for about seven  or eight years during this period of  time more minorities were in attendance  at UT than before their grades were  better their graduation rates were  higher yet the day the Michigan cases  came down on the university of texas  decided wait a minute we've got the best  of all worlds now we're going to add  race-based affirmative action to this  that was another aha moment to me I knew  that that well I thought was a way for  us to get this issue once again before  the court and I started looking for a  plaintiff to challenge you tease  admissions policies took me about three  years I found two young women who agreed  to sue UT we sued UT in 2008  lo and behold we lost at the district  court we lost at the appellate court  appeal to the Supreme Court and the  Supreme Court took that case and we won  that case in a seven to one opinion  Justice Kagan was recused from that we  even picked up Justice Breyer and brace  yourselves justice Sotomayor agreed with  us we lost Justice Ginsburg now as you  as you can imagine what we won with  Clarence Thomas and and and Sonia  Sotomayor agreeing on a legal point what  was not we didn't win a big buffet we we  won like a little nosh it was like a  little half of a sushi roll but it was  an important sushi roll it was an  important concept and the concept that  the Supreme Court came down with and  that and that round of this litigation  was that in order for a university to  use race and ethnicity in its admissions  policies it must first and they use the  word before before turning to racial  classifications and preferences a  university must make an attempt to find  a race neutral means to achieve the  diversity that they seek ok what is what  does that mean well it means that the  University of Texas had this race  neutral means that was very effective  instead of just striking down you tease  a plan apparently there was some horse  trading among the the justices and  that's why I think we ended up with  seven agreeing to this new higher  standard they remanded the case back to  the Fifth Circuit where we made  additional arguments where we lost again  and now that case once again is heading  back to the Supreme Court I decided that  while we didn't argue in Fisher one nor  will we argue in Fisher to that the time  has come for the Supreme Court to  completely end racial classifications  and preferences I started laying the  groundwork for a new round of litigation  and to that end myself and a legal team  and all of my pals that get these  midnight phone calls from me with crazy  ideas we identified three schools we  identified harvard university the  university of north carolina at chapel  hill and we identified the University of  Wisconsin at Madison so I needed three  kids who have been who were rejected  from those three schools so I could have  three lawsuits I did this you know you  just can't start cold calling for kids  although I've tried that before so I am  I launched three websites a Harvard not  fair Oh RG UNC not fair Oh RG + UW not  fair Oh RG and because by this time you  know having had four cases of the  Supreme Court the launch of these  websites dig got some notoriety and the  lunch was covered by the times and the  New York Times and other newspapers and  over a two-week period I got probably  about 300 kids signed up on these  websites and lo and behold after flying  out to California a few times going to  North Carolina a few times but  unfortunately not going to Wisconsin I  found a group of kids who had been  denied admission to Harvard and a group  of kids who had been small group kids  who have been denied admission to UNC  and we filed a lawsuit challenging both  schools on November the 17th  the harvard law suit of course is the  one that has the highest profile because  our our plaintiffs at harvard are asian  and we argue that Harvard harbored has a  hard strict intractable quota on asians  today much like they had a hard strict  quota on jewish students in the 1920s  30s and 40s that lawsuit is underway our  first status conference with Harvard  will take place next month in Boston  Harvard as you can imagine has really  wired up they they just didn't go to you  know a really sharp litigation team  instead they went straight to President  Bill Clinton's former Solicitor General  Seth Waxman who has argued uh probably  about 50 cases before the US Supreme  Court and his his supreme court  litigation team so I think the the  speculation is that regardless of what  happens in the lower courts one of these  two cases and I'm hopeful to bring a  third anybody from Wisconsin knowing  that you know one of these cases will  eventually get to the Supreme Court and  Harvard specifically wanted to have  their Supreme Court advocates sort of  you know X square one if that is the  eventualities so I'm going to encourage  you to follow these lawsuits I make  periodic postings to our website project  on fair representation orgy you can  follow this I assume that if Fisher  Fisher to is taken up by the Supreme  Court in in early May then all of this  just becomes this  kind of bubbling booyah base of  affirmative action and it's my hope that  really between the Fisher case and some  of these new cases we will finally sort  of achieve what you know the great  founders and visionaries of the civil  rights movement taught us and that is  your race in your ethnicity really  shouldn't count for anything whether  it's in college admissions or jobs
 

